<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palace of Nestor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nestor's Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> is a Mycenaean palace site on the hill of Epano Englianos in Messenia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, about 17 kilometres north of Pylos, traditionally identified with the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s King Nestor at &quot;sandy Pylos&quot;. A two-storey building with storerooms, workshops, baths and drains, destroyed by fire, it is the best-preserved Mycenaean palace yet discovered. Excavations by the University of Cincinnati and the Greek Archaeological Service, begun in 1939, found more than a thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets, part of a royal archive showing that it was the administrative centre of Mycenaean Messenia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palace-of-nestor.jpg" alt="Palace of Nestor" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Housen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20190508_088_pylos_nestor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
